      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Clinical presentation and symptoms of coronary artery disease may vary according to gender. This is not true to the same extent for acute coronary syndromes. The diagnosis is more complex in women because they show less often a typical clinical picture. In spite of these gender-related clinical differences, no diagnostic strategy in the detection of the coronary artery disease is recommended for women. Because \"standard\" treadmill or bicycle ECG stress test has a low specificity in women, non-invasive imaging (stress echocardiography, myocardial scintigraphy or cardiac magnetic resonance) should be preferred for the evaluation of myocardial ischemia in women."},
